WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:03.634
(serious hip hop music)

00:00:06.063 --> 00:00:08.450
- Well welcome to another
episode of The Addy Hour,

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:09.860
we've had a little bit of hiatus,

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.870
but excited to jump into
these conversations again,

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:14.510
and today we're gonna be talking about

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:17.900
race, politics, religion,
and mental health in America.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.410
So some heavy topics,
but some important topics

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:21.960
and really excited for two guests

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.150
that I have on the program today.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.800
The first that I'd like to introduce,

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.460
I'm honored to be able to welcome

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:30.440
professor Eddie Glaude
Jr. to The Addy Hour.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.680
As many of you probably already know,

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:36.090
Dr. Glaude is one of the
nation's most prominent scholars.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:38.660
He's an author, a political commentator,

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.360
a public intellectual,
and a passionate educator

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.140
who examines the complex dynamics

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.260
of the American experience.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.310
Dr. Glaude is the James S. McDonnell

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:48.990
distinguished university professor

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.340
and chair of the department

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.590
of African-American studies at Princeton.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.700
He's a Morehouse graduate who also holds

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.700
a master's degree in
African-American studies from Temple

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.090
and a PhD in religion from Princeton.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:03.790
Dr. Glaude is someone who is
a columnist for Time magazine,

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:05.370
he's also a regular contributor

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.390
on programs like Morning Joe,

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:10.020
Deadline: White House with Nicolle Wallace

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.490
and Meet the Press.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.240
And he's someone who
continually challenges us

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.660
to examine our collective
American conscience,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.360
and in his words, to not to
posit the greatness of America,

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.400
but to establish the ground upon which

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.490
to imagine the country anew.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.640
So I'm honored and thrilled
to be able to welcome

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.860
professor Glaude to The
Addy Hour this morning.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:28.860
Thanks for being here.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.810
- It's my pleasure, looking forward to it.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.410
- Looking forward to it as well.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.170
My next guest I'd like to introduce

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:38.270
is none other than A.D. Lumkile Thomason,

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:42.270
who is a speaker, poet, and
an award winning filmmaker.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.030
He's spoken around the United States

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:46.470
and in places like Sudan, South Africa,

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.850
China, and Palestine, and
he's also the author of

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:53.570
Permission to be Black: My
Journey with Jay Z and Jesus,

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:55.240
where he acknowledges and confronts

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:58.240
the reality of racial
trauma and shares his story

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.740
to give permission to readers
to be black, to be Christian,

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.660
and to be the person
God has made them to be.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:06.430
A.D. is a native of Detroit,

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.530
he has more than 17 years of experience

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.820
preaching and teaching the peace of Jesus

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:14.050
in ethnically divided countries,
cultures, and communities,

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:15.820
and so I'm honored to be able to welcome

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.110
A.D. to the podcast today,
thanks for being here.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:19.120
- It's good to be here.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.753
Excited.

00:02:20.753 --> 00:02:22.030
- I know we were joking offline too,

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.530
I didn't get the memo about the
coordination with the shirt,

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.837
so I'm still feeling
a little bit left out,

00:02:25.837 --> 00:02:27.230
but I think hopefully, you know,

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.770
as we roll with this conversation,

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:32.063
I'll feel a sense of belonging
and inclusion as well.

00:02:32.063 --> 00:02:34.740
(all chuckling)

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.680
Just, you know, just
to start out, you know,

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.480
the topics obviously we're
gonna be talking about

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.030
are heavy, and these are
topics that you all talk about

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.210
on a regular basis, and I
really appreciate the way

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.510
both of you are public scholars

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:47.840
and have really helped us
to continue to think about

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.000
our history as a nation,
our present and our future.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.230
But one of the things I just
want to do to start out,

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:54.790
and listeners know that
we often just like to

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.280
check in and see how people are doing,

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:58.610
but I'm curious to know with the topics

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.700
that you all engage
in, how do you continue

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.470
to engage in those topics
while also taking time

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.370
to kind of watch your
own emotional wellness,

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:09.290
balance your time management,
work-life balance,

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:10.123
all those things.

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:13.100
How do you that on a day to day basis?

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.063
Dr. Glaude, if we could
start out with you?

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.690
- It's a challenge, you know,

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:23.690
because I find myself having to comment,

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:27.113
almost every day, on the state
of the country, you know.

00:03:28.369 --> 00:03:30.320
You know, I'm being asked
to interpret the drums,

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:31.593
as Adolph Reed would say.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:36.240
And so, you know, in my work I talk about

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.610
finding your elsewhere,
a place that allows you

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:44.410
to get the requisite distance
from the reality of the world.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:45.330
And so, you know, you gotta have

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:46.930
those people who love you unconditionally.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:50.030
So I just flew down to Memphis
and hung out with my boys

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:53.260
that I've been, I've known
since my Morehouse days,

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.343
we all just met there and just
had an extraordinary time.

00:03:57.499 --> 00:03:59.750
You know, I disappear into novels.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.110
I'm finishing, I just
finished a wonderful novel

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:05.080
by Anna Burns entitled Milkman,

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:06.890
and then I re-read The
Parable of the Sower

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:09.240
by Octavia Butler, and
now I'm finishing up

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.900
Shuggie Bain by Douglas
Stuart, so you know,

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.040
just kind of disappear
into fiction in some ways.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:21.040
So, you know, finding a respite
in friends and in novels,

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:23.580
that's been the way I've been

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:24.910
keeping my head on my shoulders.

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:25.743
- That's great.

00:04:25.743 --> 00:04:26.960
And it sounds so intentional too.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.270
I mean, even the time that
you spend reading books,

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:30.630
hanging out with folks,
I imagine, you know,

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.800
that takes time too, so
I'm impressed that you're,

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.150
you carve out the time for that.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:37.120
Do you ever get any pushback
in terms of, you know,

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.720
why are you not available
for this and that?

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.628
Or, I mean, how have you navigated that?

00:04:40.628 --> 00:04:42.039
- Yeah, I mean, you know,
everybody, you know,

00:04:42.039 --> 00:04:45.120
once you acquire a certain
kind of public standing,

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.940
you know, everybody thinks that they,

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:48.500
everyone's asking for your time.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:51.130
And so you just have to be very deliberate

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:55.170
about being able to
carve out your own time

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:57.793
and being able to cultivate
the capacity to say no.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:00.920
And I needed, you know,

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:03.130
because I'm the chair of
a department, you know,

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:05.890
I'm public, you know, I'm
traveling around the country,

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:08.260
I'm doing television, I'm writing.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:09.990
I just needed, you know,
you gotta replenish

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:11.850
because this is not a sprint.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.340
This is a long distance race.

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.600
So you gotta be able to at least

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:16.800
carve out some time for yourself.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.330
So some people might be
annoyed when I say no,

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:22.140
but you know, we try to
say it in a generous way.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:25.040
Like I was, like I got
home training, you know.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:26.223
- Yeah, I think that's so important.

00:05:26.223 --> 00:05:28.770
I think that's encouraging
for others to hear too,

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:31.190
'cause I think sometimes
people look at folks like you

00:05:31.190 --> 00:05:32.040
and they're like, how did they do that?

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.060
How did they balance that all?

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:33.893
How do they keep going?

00:05:33.893 --> 00:05:36.270
So to be able to hear kind
of that behind the scenes

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.160
and know there are times
that you're saying no

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.460
and really focusing on what you need to do

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:43.100
to stay in that place, I
think that's really important.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.000
- Absolutely, doc.

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:46.150
- A.D., what about you?

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.870
- Yeah, I mean, it's, I'll make it,

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:52.070
I'll simplify it to just
say I try to connect

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:55.520
with nature in silence.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:59.570
To me silence rejuvenates me, you know,

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:02.040
so some tangible things I'll do,

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:05.450
I'll go fishing and turn
the phone off, you know,

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:08.820
and I could be out there
from sun up to sun down

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:13.100
just in my thoughts, 12 hours a day.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.830
I got a bow, so I do bow hunting

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:18.960
and I'm out there doing
archery in the woods

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.840
and target and it's good.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.170
It's, you know, I do read,
I like film, you know,

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:29.320
like that fiction, that can remind me of

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:33.450
the possibility of what
could be despite what is,

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.573
you know, so aspirational things.

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:37.830
And yeah, you got to connect with people

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:38.790
who have a like-mind,

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.530
but they don't want anything from you.

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:44.380
I think that's the rare reality

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:46.770
is finding people who don't want anything

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:48.960
or won't turn the
conversation in their favor,

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.350
but they just like your presence.

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:54.360
So those are probably the
things that I would do.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.356
Or I do.
- That's definitely good.

00:06:56.356 --> 00:06:58.060
Is that something that
you've done from the get-go

00:06:58.060 --> 00:06:59.260
or were these things that you'd say

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:02.270
you developed over time out of necessity?

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:05.053
- Definitely over time out of necessity.

00:07:06.530 --> 00:07:08.633
You know, you look at different characters

00:07:08.633 --> 00:07:13.260
in the history from, you know,

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.790
you take the Dalai Lama
going on in creation

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:20.240
or Gandhi, King, you know, Christ himself.

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:23.123
You just see, there's something
with creation in silence.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:29.450
You know, and like, you
know, we'll say it earlier,

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:35.100
like, you know, people will
steal that time if you let them.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.680
So you have to have a discipline,

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:41.300
a muscle of saying no in a
kind way to replenish yourself.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.350
- Yeah, I think that's so good.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:45.360
I wanted to bring it home to
students a little bit too,

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:46.580
'cause you know just in my experience

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:49.250
working with students, I
mean I'm always encouraged

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:50.990
by their passion and how willing they are

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:53.690
to jump in to so many important topics

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:57.470
and conversations and
fights, to be honest,

00:07:57.470 --> 00:07:58.570
in a lot of ways.

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:00.070
And I see that really with the, you know,

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:01.220
the generation coming up.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:03.110
But then at the same time sometimes,

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:04.450
like you all were both talking about,

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.860
I don't always see the
willingness to say no.

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:10.070
And so people get themselves
spread really thin

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:12.440
and especially with the emotional
toll that comes with it,

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:14.597
not always having time
to kind of step back

00:08:14.597 --> 00:08:15.740
and take care of themselves,

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:18.400
or if mental health challenges come up

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:19.600
really not having the tools

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.820
to address those types of things.

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:24.250
I'm curious, not even
just in terms of timing

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.520
but in terms of topics, how do you all

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.680
decide what topics to engage in

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:29.797
and when to pull back and say, okay,

00:08:29.797 --> 00:08:31.540
you know, I'm passionate about this

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:32.770
but this is something that right now

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:35.880
I just need to make sure I
don't spread myself too thin

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:37.570
and just go a thousand miles an hour,

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:40.523
or like pick which battles to engage in?

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.330
Professor Glaude, you want to?

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:48.110
- You know I, you know, the
temptation in this world,

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:49.740
particularly in the world of punditry,

00:08:49.740 --> 00:08:54.390
is that you can speak
on everything, you know,

00:08:54.390 --> 00:08:58.520
and you know, A.D.'s point about silence

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:00.230
is really, really key, right?

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:02.600
Sometimes you just have to, you know,

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:05.203
understand that this is not your lane.

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:11.430
You know, I can talk about
this, I have opinions on it,

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:14.940
but this is really not
where my expertise lies,

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:16.343
so why am I talking?

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:18.133
Right?

00:09:18.133 --> 00:09:21.060
And so a lot of times I
find myself, you know,

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:26.060
making decisions based upon, you know,

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:30.710
somebody else could be, somebody
else might be better here.

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:34.180
Why do I need to occupy this space?

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:35.013
Right?

00:09:35.013 --> 00:09:35.846
Open it up for someone else.

00:09:35.846 --> 00:09:37.680
Let someone else step in there.

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:41.490
So, you know, I make
decisions on the one hand,

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:44.300
you know, around what I know.

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:46.930
What's my value-add to the conversation?

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:49.180
How might I frame it different?

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:52.130
On the other hand, and then
there's another, you know,

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:56.060
what, I'm not a guy who wants
to talk about, you know,

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:57.530
popular culture a lot, right?

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.760
So, you know, I'm not
the one you're gonna call

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.530
to talk about Jay Z or DMX
or, you know, or Cardi B.

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:07.000
That's not my zone, right?

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:09.361
So again, knowing where you are, right?

00:10:09.361 --> 00:10:12.560
So what does it mean when I'm being asked

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:14.498
to reflect on a movie or a film

00:10:14.498 --> 00:10:17.470
or a popular cultural event?

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:19.980
Understanding where I am
and trying to be consistent

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:21.040
with who I am, right?

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:23.520
So I think part of it
has everything to do,

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.060
both of those points have everything to do

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:28.616
with being consistent, trying
to be consistently clear

00:10:28.616 --> 00:10:33.616
about the purpose of your
voice and why you're speaking.

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:37.050
What is your platform for?

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:39.490
How does it relate to your calling?

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:42.120
And when you ask yourself those questions,

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:45.140
then you're not pursuing opportunities

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:47.493
in order to cultivate brand.
- That's good.

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:50.536
- You see, 'cause you're doing something

00:10:50.536 --> 00:10:53.273
that is more than just simply
you running your mouth.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:58.020
So that becomes the basis of
making these sorts of choices.

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:00.920
'Cause if not, somebody asks
you to talk about anything,

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:03.490
anything and everything, and you know,

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:04.710
it's this, something
happens to black people,

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:06.711
what do you think black people think?

00:11:06.711 --> 00:11:08.460
(all chuckling)
Right?

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:10.640
So, 'cause you're the
interpreter of black folk,

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:13.850
you know, so you've gotta be
very deliberate in this regard.

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:14.843
- Yeah, I think that's so good

00:11:14.843 --> 00:11:16.650
from the standpoint of the speaker.

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:18.250
I'm a little bit curious
because how do we,

00:11:18.250 --> 00:11:19.590
I'm curious what you
think about how people

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:22.520
should filter that as
consumers, in a sense,

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:24.380
'cause as a speaker you have control

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:27.180
about whether you step
into a conversation or not.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:28.430
Granted there are a lot of people

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:29.680
who are stepping out of their lane

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:31.910
and still speaking into conversations.

00:11:31.910 --> 00:11:34.120
So from the receiving end, like how do we,

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:35.060
how do we filter that?

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:38.040
And just not get taken up by everything

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:40.200
that everyone has said
about every single topic?

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:42.240
- Well, you know, this
is one of the challenges,

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:43.073
I'll just say this real quickly

00:11:43.073 --> 00:11:44.010
and then I'll shut up, right?

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:45.960
One of the challenges
about our current moment

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:49.140
is that there's a kind of
leveling that happens, right?

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:51.110
The kind of disappearance of expertise,

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:54.620
and so because of social
media platforms and the like,

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:57.707
everybody can be an expert
on anything and everything.

00:11:57.707 --> 00:12:01.438
And so you have to be a kind
of discerning listener, right?

00:12:01.438 --> 00:12:03.820
How do you absorb information?

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:07.460
How do you, who do you
extend authority to, right?

00:12:07.460 --> 00:12:10.260
What material are you drawing on

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:11.690
in order to form your opinion?

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:15.460
So I think part of what we
have to do in this moment,

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:18.380
where seriousness is under attack,

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:22.770
is cultivate the habits of
listening intelligently, right?

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:27.180
Cultivate the habits of acquiring

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:32.060
a kind of ruthless orientation
to what you take in,

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:37.750
because as we're taking in
all this information, man,

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:39.793
we're also taking in a lot of toxins.

00:12:40.678 --> 00:12:44.710
I mean you on Twitter for just 15 minutes

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:47.100
and you just kind like,
damn, I need to go brush my,

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:49.430
I need to go take a bath or something,

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:51.310
'cause there's just a lot of negativity

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:54.250
just kind of coming at you
over and over and over again.

00:12:54.250 --> 00:12:57.170
So there's, the implication
of that leveling is that,

00:12:57.170 --> 00:12:58.960
you know, everybody thinks
they know everything,

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:02.210
when hardly anybody's reading these days

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:03.500
or they're reading at the surface.

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:06.650
So you have to become a much
more discerning listener

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:09.160
and choose who you're
going to lend authority to

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:11.090
as opposed to just
accepting everybody's voice.

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:13.360
- Yeah, yeah that's true.

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:15.490
A.D., before we swing back
just to your filtering,

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:17.520
I'm just curious, also in
your role as a professor,

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.415
'cause some of the topics
that you teach about,

00:13:19.415 --> 00:13:22.170
you know, about human rights,
decolonizing your faith,

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:23.470
filmmaking and things like that,

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:25.160
are there ways that
you guide your students

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:27.030
through that filtering process?

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:28.010
Because those topics too,

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:30.150
I'm assuming that you're deconstructing

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:32.950
a lot of presuppositions
as you get into that.

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:34.360
So I feel like a lot of these topics

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:36.870
would likely come up in
the classroom for you.

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:39.100
- Yeah, I mean, that's
a good way to say it,

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:43.070
deconstructing people's presuppositions.

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:46.257
Man, that is a hard task (laughs).

00:13:47.335 --> 00:13:52.335
'Cause when people have
presupposed and concluded,

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:57.053
they'll see that in anything,
even if it's not there.

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:01.760
So I think people have, one
they have a fear of missing out.

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:05.420
You know, and because they have
such a fear of missing out,

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:10.040
they'll take in the toxic and the healthy,

00:14:10.040 --> 00:14:11.180
but the problem is they don't know

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:14.240
how to distinguish between
the two anymore, you know?

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:16.340
So now people are becoming experts

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:20.203
just on being up on the trash, right?

00:14:20.203 --> 00:14:24.130
(laughs) You know, that's the new thing.

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:27.840
Whereas before, in our
generation, we actually wanted

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:30.800
to go to the expert because
they put in the time,

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:34.140
not because they were charismatic
or et cetera, et cetera.

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:35.770
So kind of lost that.

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:37.870
So what I challenge my students on

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:42.870
is having a grind, a work
ethic to dig, you know,

00:14:43.757 --> 00:14:46.120
and a lot of people
just don't want to dig.

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:48.230
They wanna, they have
a fear of missing out

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:49.730
and then they don't want to dig,

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:50.960
and when you got social media

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:52.870
it gives you the best of all of that.

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:55.810
When you're lazy and you
don't want to miss out,

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:59.340
you're like I just take in
whatever comes my way, right?

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:02.330
And we know that that
doesn't make a, I would call

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:07.300
an honest intellectual,
an honest pundit, right?

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:10.233
It just makes one who
becomes now personalities.

00:15:11.150 --> 00:15:12.800
And so that's what I challenge them,

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:15.400
I'll say are you gonna be
a person or a personality?

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:17.333
Yeah.

00:15:18.463 --> 00:15:20.560
- I like that.
- Yeah, that's good.

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.860
How do the students react with that?

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:23.693
- It's good, I mean it's good.

00:15:23.693 --> 00:15:26.723
It's refreshing for
them actually, you know,

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:30.793
'cause no one is challenging
them on that, you know.

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:35.330
I think what Eddie said earlier is good.

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:36.980
Who you give authority to.

00:15:36.980 --> 00:15:40.680
It's almost like, why do you give

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:42.745
your authority away so easily?

00:15:42.745 --> 00:15:46.930
(laughs) What, now tell me what they did?

00:15:46.930 --> 00:15:49.870
Oh, they got a 10 time platinum record,

00:15:49.870 --> 00:15:52.713
so that's why you follow what
they say on civil rights?

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:56.863
No!
(all laughing)

00:15:59.353 --> 00:16:01.720
Obviously I'm being
facetious, but that's what,

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:04.097
you got to show them their
broken philosophy sometimes.

00:16:04.097 --> 00:16:05.430
- Yeah, yeah.

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:06.960
That's really good.

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:08.340
To push on that a little bit though,

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:10.080
I mean I'm thinking of
the flip side of that too.

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:11.170
What do you think about, you know,

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:13.630
the situations where people are kind of

00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:15.010
sharing from their lived experience?

00:16:15.010 --> 00:16:16.620
So, you know, thinking back to LeBron

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:19.200
and the just shut up and
dribble type of comment.

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:22.020
How do we balance those two sides of it?

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:22.853
'Cause I think you're right.

00:16:22.853 --> 00:16:25.490
At one hand, if someone's
a platinum selling artist

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:28.020
it doesn't mean that they
have put in the grind

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:30.220
on certain components,
but at the same time,

00:16:30.220 --> 00:16:31.580
we have the flip side
where people are just

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:36.180
invalidating people speaking
into these social issues

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:37.071
when they, I mean, how,

00:16:37.071 --> 00:16:39.380
what do you all think about that line?

00:16:39.380 --> 00:16:41.150
How do we balance that?

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:44.380
- All right, let me
give a Dwayne Wade live

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:45.850
to Eddie on this one.

00:16:45.850 --> 00:16:50.560
So I am not a proponent
of shut up and dribble

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:52.340
in its connotation,

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:54.920
but I am when you haven't done the work.

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:56.410
And I'm gonna tell you why.

00:16:56.410 --> 00:17:01.410
Because what happens is they'll
give authority to a LeBron

00:17:01.840 --> 00:17:04.660
who hasn't read anything

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:07.300
and then millions of people
start walking it out.

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.510
Like, yeah man, see what we gotta do is

00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:11.860
we just gotta say F the police, man,

00:17:11.860 --> 00:17:13.230
you know not that LeBron said that,

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:14.327
but when people start talking

00:17:14.327 --> 00:17:16.650
and they haven't done the actual work,

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:19.570
then you're swaying culture in a way

00:17:19.570 --> 00:17:21.053
with that lack of work ethic.

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:24.660
- So, you know, just to pick up that,

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:26.271
'cause I'm gonna take
it and do this with it.

00:17:26.271 --> 00:17:28.854
(all laughing)

00:17:30.278 --> 00:17:34.950
There are examples of courage
that abound in everyday life,

00:17:36.530 --> 00:17:40.000
and people who have a lot to lose

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:44.113
when they exhibit that
courage is worth lifting up.

00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:47.260
So when someone says shut up and dribble

00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:52.260
and you respond, that is a moment, right?

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:56.590
Where, you know, LeBron James thought more

00:17:56.590 --> 00:18:00.550
about himself as a man, as a human being,

00:18:00.550 --> 00:18:03.963
than himself as a kind of
product, as a basketball player.

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:08.768
But it doesn't follow from that, right,

00:18:08.768 --> 00:18:11.760
that now LeBron or Jay Z for that matter

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:16.760
can step into, you know,
the, the brouhaha over,

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:21.310
for example, the national
anthem and the NFL.

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:23.790
And suddenly because you are a businessman

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:27.170
and you have this cachet that
you can enter into that space

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:30.950
and suddenly resolve everything
that Colin Kaepernick

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:32.430
was trying to do.

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:34.743
And the folk around him
were trying to do, right?

00:18:34.743 --> 00:18:36.350
So I think it's really important

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:38.200
just to pick up on A.D.'s point right,

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:42.703
that examples of courage are important,

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:47.270
but they come, it also comes with,

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:50.500
when we talk about moving
from a moment to a movement,

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:54.810
it also requires a kind
of depth of learning.

00:18:54.810 --> 00:18:58.620
So, you know, and people are influential

00:18:58.620 --> 00:18:59.820
in a number of different areas,

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:02.760
whether they're in sport,
whether they're in academe,

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:05.460
whether they're in the business world,

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:07.910
whether there's your uncle or your aunt,

00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:11.490
folks are influential, but
it doesn't necessarily,

00:19:11.490 --> 00:19:13.875
just because they're
influential in one moment

00:19:13.875 --> 00:19:15.960
doesn't mean that they're
influential in all moments.

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:16.990
- See, that's good.

00:19:18.180 --> 00:19:22.574
You catch that?
(all laughing)

00:19:22.574 --> 00:19:23.797
Did you catch that?

00:19:23.797 --> 00:19:26.330
And I'm gonna, again, I'm
gonna do another move,

00:19:26.330 --> 00:19:27.323
I'll pass it back.

00:19:28.646 --> 00:19:32.577
And here's what I think people
miss in what he's saying,

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:38.630
we don't pass the ball to
the right person anymore.

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:43.340
We assume we're supposed
to take the shot, right?

00:19:43.340 --> 00:19:44.173
You see what I'm saying?

00:19:44.173 --> 00:19:47.380
So if I know Eddie has put in his work

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:50.720
and they ask him something
on politics, I go,

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:53.250
yeah, I can give
insightful, but I go, nah,

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:54.340
I'm gonna pass it to him.

00:19:54.340 --> 00:19:58.070
And we don't see people in those platforms

00:19:58.070 --> 00:20:02.590
have the right relationships
to pass to the right person

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:04.790
or at least be mentored
by the right person

00:20:04.790 --> 00:20:06.340
that they should be passing to.

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:08.633
Right?

00:20:08.633 --> 00:20:11.760
So my question be like why isn't a LeBron

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:14.380
being mentored by the
Eddie Glaudes in the world

00:20:14.380 --> 00:20:16.290
so when they ask him that
question, he's like, bam,

00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:17.253
I know what to do.

00:20:18.910 --> 00:20:20.770
- Well, you know, I would hope

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:22.750
that he has some folks in his camp.

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:24.200
I pray that he does.
(all chuckling)

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:25.580
But you know, I think it's really,

00:20:25.580 --> 00:20:26.890
I think that's a really important point.

00:20:26.890 --> 00:20:29.430
It goes back to something
I said earlier, A.D.,

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:34.430
and that, you know, the
temptation is to hog the mic.

00:20:36.458 --> 00:20:38.480
That, you know, you think you can,

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:41.430
just because you got it you
suppose to speak on everything.

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:46.690
And the discipline, the
spiritual discipline actually,

00:20:46.690 --> 00:20:50.860
to actually be quiet and pass the mic

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:52.700
is something that we have to cultivate.

00:20:52.700 --> 00:20:55.010
- That's good.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:20:55.010 --> 00:20:58.883
- I like that.
- Wow, that's so true.

00:20:59.830 --> 00:21:02.120
I think the challenge
is actually cultivating,

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:05.000
getting to that point,
helping people cultivate that.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:07.760
I mean, I think it's refreshing

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:10.297
to hear both of you espouse that here.

00:21:10.297 --> 00:21:12.000
I'm just, I guess I'm trying to,

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:13.910
how does that work out practically?

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:14.743
- Let me give you an example,

00:21:14.743 --> 00:21:17.350
let me give you a practical example.

00:21:17.350 --> 00:21:20.610
Bob Moses, God bless his soul,

00:21:20.610 --> 00:21:22.480
you know, I wouldn't be here

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:25.853
if it wasn't for what Bob
Moses did in Mississippi,

00:21:26.770 --> 00:21:27.740
you know, I'm a country boy

00:21:27.740 --> 00:21:29.730
from Moss Point, Mississippi.

00:21:29.730 --> 00:21:33.480
SNCC was in Moss point, Hollis Watkins,

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:35.630
I mean, Stokely
Carmichael, all these folk,

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:39.263
and Ms. Ella Baker told the SNCC folk,

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:42.020
you know, shut up and listen,

00:21:42.020 --> 00:21:44.340
maybe you might learn something.

00:21:44.340 --> 00:21:46.323
As they were organizing sharecrop.

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:52.343
And so Bob's orientation
was to entrance into spaces

00:21:53.300 --> 00:21:55.410
and create the conditions under which

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:58.120
indigenous leadership would emerge,

00:21:58.120 --> 00:21:58.953
'cause he was saying

00:21:58.953 --> 00:22:01.400
we are the leaders we've been looking for.

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:03.770
And so at the heart of his politics

00:22:03.770 --> 00:22:05.183
is a politics of tending.

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:08.550
Tending to the person
right in front of you.

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:11.070
Tending to the situation
right in front of you,

00:22:11.070 --> 00:22:12.950
which means you have to have
the habits of listening,

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:15.730
you have to be perceptive,
you have to be empathetic,

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:16.720
all of these things.

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:21.720
So at the heart of SNCC, as
a civil rights organization,

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:25.320
right, is moving into
spaces that people thought

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:28.820
were at the margins of American
democratic life, right?

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:31.350
And creating the conditions under which

00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:33.710
a Fannie Lou Hamer could emerge.

00:22:33.710 --> 00:22:35.770
- Come on.
- You see what I mean?

00:22:35.770 --> 00:22:37.660
So part of what we have to do,

00:22:37.660 --> 00:22:41.760
oftentimes in this culture
and this moment now,

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:44.880
we're so preoccupied with
our own individual brands,

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:47.213
we're so preoccupied with projecting,

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:50.420
or as we would say getting
something on consignment

00:22:50.420 --> 00:22:52.470
so we can then get our hustle.

00:22:52.470 --> 00:22:54.913
I'm going a little too deep there-

00:22:54.913 --> 00:22:56.187
- No, go man (laughs).

00:22:56.187 --> 00:22:59.850
- Give me this so that I
can do this, and you know,

00:22:59.850 --> 00:23:02.680
'cause we all want to
get in the game, right?

00:23:02.680 --> 00:23:04.770
Not understanding what really,

00:23:04.770 --> 00:23:07.933
what we're trying to do really
is change the game entirely.

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:11.520
And that's gonna require not folk

00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:14.460
getting out front necessarily,

00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:16.760
but all of us kind of
creating the conditions

00:23:16.760 --> 00:23:19.270
under which each and every other person

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:20.970
can become the fullest human being

00:23:20.970 --> 00:23:23.380
that they're called to
be, if that makes sense.

00:23:23.380 --> 00:23:24.393
- No that's good.

00:23:24.393 --> 00:23:26.870
So to answer your question
directly, 'cause I-

00:23:26.870 --> 00:23:29.030
- Alley-oop.
(all laughing)

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:30.650
- He wants some tangibles,

00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:32.490
I can see the neural science in his brain,

00:23:32.490 --> 00:23:34.114
he's like man I need some 10 steps.

00:23:34.114 --> 00:23:37.143
(all laughing)
- Can't hide it.

00:23:38.290 --> 00:23:43.290
- You know, I think we've been
fortunate to know our story,

00:23:43.330 --> 00:23:46.220
to look at our story, what
are some resonating things?

00:23:46.220 --> 00:23:48.630
So if I were to say
something to people I'd say

00:23:48.630 --> 00:23:49.520
write out your story.

00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:50.840
Where did you grow up?

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:52.320
Mom, dad, what's their story?

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:55.830
Siblings, no siblings, the environment,

00:23:55.830 --> 00:23:59.950
if you fortunate, college,
you know, masters, doctorate.

00:23:59.950 --> 00:24:02.850
Write out, and you'll start to see themes.

00:24:02.850 --> 00:24:05.493
And going back to Eddie's point earlier,

00:24:06.420 --> 00:24:08.340
when you see those themes,

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:11.123
