just the danger, just the
danger of living in that space

00:35:43.730 --> 00:35:45.280
and I'm sure, maybe
we'll come back to that

00:35:45.280 --> 00:35:47.780
in terms of the commentary
on the country too.

00:35:47.780 --> 00:35:50.030
But A.D. I want to give you
a chance to kind of respond

00:35:50.030 --> 00:35:53.581
to what, all this truth that
Eddie's dropping as well.

00:35:53.581 --> 00:35:54.590
(all laughing)

00:35:54.590 --> 00:35:57.670
- Yeah, no, I mean that,
I can speak from that.

00:35:57.670 --> 00:36:01.690
First of all the Nikki Giovanni
James Baldwin interview,

00:36:01.690 --> 00:36:05.800
that is like on the top five-
(all laughing)

00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:09.333
Just pieces that you need to consume.

00:36:10.470 --> 00:36:11.440
It was phenomenal.

00:36:11.440 --> 00:36:14.410
And like he said, with
Baldwin being Baldwin

00:36:14.410 --> 00:36:16.210
and she being the up and coming,

00:36:16.210 --> 00:36:18.520
but she challenged him.

00:36:18.520 --> 00:36:22.520
Showed me a lot about who she still is

00:36:22.520 --> 00:36:25.160
and who we need to be as people,

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:28.650
like don't get caught
up in this personality

00:36:28.650 --> 00:36:31.250
but look at who they are.

00:36:31.250 --> 00:36:34.840
Is this right, is this wrong,
does it need to be challenged?

00:36:34.840 --> 00:36:37.511
And I think, you know, going to his point,

00:36:37.511 --> 00:36:42.511
we take on so many, I would
say lies and deceptions

00:36:43.140 --> 00:36:46.510
that we aren't strong
enough to run into the thing

00:36:46.510 --> 00:36:48.370
we fear most and come out

00:36:48.370 --> 00:36:50.520
and to make it out on the other end.

00:36:50.520 --> 00:36:53.443
I can't tell you the
number of people who say,

00:36:54.360 --> 00:36:56.520
yeah man, I get what you saying A.D.,

00:36:56.520 --> 00:36:58.320
about, you know, facing the fears

00:36:58.320 --> 00:36:59.980
and you gotta deal with the trauma,

00:36:59.980 --> 00:37:02.677
but I don't know if I'm gonna
make it on the other side.

00:37:02.677 --> 00:37:06.980
And my response is, what
a low view of yourself

00:37:06.980 --> 00:37:08.480
and what a low view of humanity

00:37:08.480 --> 00:37:11.280
that you don't believe
you have the strength

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:14.630
to make it through this pain

00:37:14.630 --> 00:37:17.653
that isn't supposed to be
the marker of who you are.

00:37:19.265 --> 00:37:22.410
So I'll go, if I'm telling you, yo,

00:37:22.410 --> 00:37:24.040
you can actually heal from this.

00:37:24.040 --> 00:37:27.450
Yeah, but I'm used to work
working with this broken arm.

00:37:27.450 --> 00:37:29.730
But you know it can be healed and better

00:37:29.730 --> 00:37:31.460
if we break it, and I get that.

00:37:31.460 --> 00:37:32.630
But I don't know if I'm
gonna make it through

00:37:32.630 --> 00:37:35.700
the other side of that,
the other side of the pain.

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:38.230
And a lot of times I'm thinking, wow,

00:37:38.230 --> 00:37:42.891
what has convinced us of that?

00:37:42.891 --> 00:37:45.510
That's a huge presupposition.

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:48.360
And so I'm always challenging
people to be more inspired,

00:37:48.360 --> 00:37:51.970
to be more aspiring of themselves

00:37:51.970 --> 00:37:55.150
and to reject these false narratives that,

00:37:55.150 --> 00:37:58.010
this low place or low station that we have

00:37:58.010 --> 00:37:59.610
is where we're supposed to just

00:37:59.610 --> 00:38:04.120
survive through life, you know?

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:06.150
- Yeah, yeah.

00:38:06.150 --> 00:38:07.270
Surviving versus thriving.

00:38:07.270 --> 00:38:09.270
But it is not easy, as you mentioned too,

00:38:09.270 --> 00:38:10.863
just, and we'll get back to some of the,

00:38:10.863 --> 00:38:12.270
what you brought in your book too,

00:38:12.270 --> 00:38:15.580
but just all the traumas
that people go through

00:38:15.580 --> 00:38:18.450
and just all the lack of
control that comes with that.

00:38:18.450 --> 00:38:20.140
So in some ways I think sometimes there is

00:38:20.140 --> 00:38:22.710
a false sense of safety, and
not minimizing it at all,

00:38:22.710 --> 00:38:26.000
but a false sense of safety
in not moving forward

00:38:26.000 --> 00:38:27.250
because at least then people feel like

00:38:27.250 --> 00:38:30.220
they can control that aspect,

00:38:30.220 --> 00:38:32.400
versus going through to a place

00:38:32.400 --> 00:38:33.870
where they may not have control,

00:38:33.870 --> 00:38:35.650
again, all the challenges
come through that.

00:38:35.650 --> 00:38:37.030
I mean, there's obviously ways

00:38:37.030 --> 00:38:37.967
that trauma counselors and folks

00:38:37.967 --> 00:38:38.990
can help people through that,

00:38:38.990 --> 00:38:40.850
but I think that's a key piece as well,

00:38:40.850 --> 00:38:43.590
but I think it's so important
that you bring that to people

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:45.040
and show what is on the other side,

00:38:45.040 --> 00:38:48.000
'cause it gets back to
that idea of the pain too,

00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:51.120
and how much stronger we are
when we go through the pain.

00:38:51.120 --> 00:38:52.800
I'm gonna stay in my
lane in the neuroscience

00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:56.950
and not try and venture out,
but there is truth to it.

00:38:56.950 --> 00:38:58.350
- Say one thing on that?

00:38:58.350 --> 00:39:03.350
I think also, I'll speak
directly to, you know,

00:39:03.400 --> 00:39:06.070
African-American descendants of slaves.

00:39:06.070 --> 00:39:08.560
I will speak directly to this.

00:39:08.560 --> 00:39:13.560
We downplay the fact that
we tangibly made it through

00:39:13.660 --> 00:39:18.660
what I would call some of the
most hellacious, terrorizing,

00:39:18.660 --> 00:39:21.630
and inhumane tangible things.

00:39:21.630 --> 00:39:25.190
We don't even have to talk
about the actual morality of it,

00:39:25.190 --> 00:39:28.180
I'm just talking about physically.

00:39:28.180 --> 00:39:30.800
And that came to light
when I was able to do

00:39:30.800 --> 00:39:33.710
this documentary in Ghana and I physically

00:39:33.710 --> 00:39:36.780
were in these places
where you couldn't see

00:39:36.780 --> 00:39:39.110
the light of day for months on end,

00:39:39.110 --> 00:39:40.680
we're talking about a different level

00:39:40.680 --> 00:39:44.580
of solitary confinement, right?

00:39:44.580 --> 00:39:46.675
You stand there and you go,

00:39:46.675 --> 00:39:51.190
there's no way there should
be a physical person,

00:39:51.190 --> 00:39:53.330
we ain't even got to the boats,

00:39:53.330 --> 00:39:55.910
we're talking about the
processing castles, right?

00:39:55.910 --> 00:39:58.440
And then you go before
the processing castles

00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:01.584
the 500 mile journey
and you go, we had to,

00:40:01.584 --> 00:40:04.043
we, our, my ancestors did this too?

00:40:05.460 --> 00:40:08.483
So when you come through all that you go,

00:40:09.850 --> 00:40:11.380
I don't know what it is,

00:40:11.380 --> 00:40:13.210
I don't know how I'm gonna get there,

00:40:13.210 --> 00:40:15.350
but I've been born to triumph.

00:40:15.350 --> 00:40:20.350
My people, my specific
people, are born to triumph.

00:40:21.220 --> 00:40:25.290
It may be Jay Z, we may not
know what the tools are,

00:40:25.290 --> 00:40:30.263
but I do know this: I'm born to triumph.

00:40:31.563 --> 00:40:35.170
And that's something, as
I look at us as brown men,

00:40:35.170 --> 00:40:38.823
I'm like, nah, I ain't believing that lie.

00:40:41.230 --> 00:40:42.063
- Yeah.

00:40:42.983 --> 00:40:44.943
For real, for real, real talk.

00:40:45.890 --> 00:40:46.733
- Real talk.

00:40:48.250 --> 00:40:50.422
- Eddie, anything you want to follow up?

00:40:50.422 --> 00:40:51.450
That powerful statement?

00:40:51.450 --> 00:40:53.100
- Yeah, you know, I used to say to my son,

00:40:53.100 --> 00:40:55.297
I didn't used to say,
I say it all the time

00:40:55.297 --> 00:40:56.440
and I say it to myself:

00:40:56.440 --> 00:40:58.423
the world conspires to make you small.

00:41:00.620 --> 00:41:02.250
And the question you have to ask yourself

00:41:02.250 --> 00:41:03.603
is will you be complicit?

00:41:04.820 --> 00:41:05.717
- Come on (laughs).

00:41:07.816 --> 00:41:09.890
- And if you're not gonna be complicit,

00:41:09.890 --> 00:41:12.783
then you must do like John
Coltrane, take giant steps.

00:41:14.520 --> 00:41:15.930
So, you know what I mean?

00:41:15.930 --> 00:41:19.823
So I think, and you know, one
way to render A.D.'s point

00:41:19.823 --> 00:41:23.530
is that we all were born with
a crown above our head, man.

00:41:23.530 --> 00:41:25.543
The question is will you grow into it?

00:41:26.830 --> 00:41:28.550
Will you grow into it?

00:41:28.550 --> 00:41:31.720
And part of the challenge
we face over and over again

00:41:31.720 --> 00:41:33.220
is getting people to see that.

00:41:34.580 --> 00:41:36.983
Because again, the world
tries to make a small.

00:41:38.060 --> 00:41:38.900
'Cause you got some folks

00:41:38.900 --> 00:41:42.190
in the most cosmopolitan city in the world

00:41:42.190 --> 00:41:45.513
can't see beyond their
borough, their neighborhood.

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:49.110
I'm not talking about folk in the Delta,

00:41:49.110 --> 00:41:51.201
I'm talking about folk in New York.

00:41:51.201 --> 00:41:52.230
Talking about folk in Chicago,

00:41:52.230 --> 00:41:53.830
talking about folk in Los Angeles, right?

00:41:53.830 --> 00:41:58.830
So there's a sense in which,
to understand one's life

00:42:00.680 --> 00:42:04.210
as the canvas to create art,
to create beauty, right,

00:42:04.210 --> 00:42:08.970
no matter the circumstance,
is a challenge, is hard to do.

00:42:08.970 --> 00:42:12.640
So how do we break through
that is the question, you know?

00:42:13.550 --> 00:42:17.360
But I have no, I don't
want to be complicit.

00:42:17.360 --> 00:42:18.940
I'm gonna sound like Melville,

00:42:18.940 --> 00:42:22.151
Melville's Bartleby the
Scrivener: I prefer not to.

00:42:22.151 --> 00:42:26.423
(all laughing)
- So good.

00:42:29.551 --> 00:42:30.960
- That's good.

00:42:30.960 --> 00:42:33.410
- I'm still, I mean, I'm
still harping back to my,

00:42:33.410 --> 00:42:34.670
just the community part and A.D.

00:42:34.670 --> 00:42:36.710
I'm still feeling that
like what are the steps,

00:42:36.710 --> 00:42:38.590
what are the steps?
(all laughing)

00:42:38.590 --> 00:42:40.640
But I think, I mean, in some ways it's a,

00:42:41.936 --> 00:42:44.253
even the way you described
that with your son,

00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:47.040
like and the influence that you mentioned

00:42:47.040 --> 00:42:49.450
that each of us have in the interactions,

00:42:49.450 --> 00:42:51.300
in our spheres of influence,

00:42:51.300 --> 00:42:54.160
and the impact of having
those conversations

00:42:54.160 --> 00:42:56.100
and building that sense of community

00:42:56.100 --> 00:42:59.743
even one person at a time
I think is so, so powerful.

00:43:01.243 --> 00:43:02.630
- Absolutely.
- Yeah.

00:43:02.630 --> 00:43:04.600
- I'm sorry, go ahead.
- No, you're good.

00:43:04.600 --> 00:43:05.990
- No, I was just thinking
about my son, right?

00:43:05.990 --> 00:43:08.930
You know, I remember we were in an airport

00:43:08.930 --> 00:43:12.620
when it came, the decision to
not charge the police officers

00:43:12.620 --> 00:43:14.093
who could killed Tamir Rice.

00:43:15.530 --> 00:43:18.380
And I immediately looked
back to find my son.

00:43:18.380 --> 00:43:22.703
Six two, he was at the time, you know,

00:43:22.703 --> 00:43:26.240
a D1 prospect for basketball,
and I'm looking at him

00:43:26.240 --> 00:43:30.483
and he's pacing around the
airport like a caged panther.

00:43:32.490 --> 00:43:33.640
And I'm trying to figure out

00:43:33.640 --> 00:43:37.853
how do I keep this rage from
taking root in his spirit?

00:43:39.730 --> 00:43:43.093
How do I keep, you know, the
anger from turning on him?

00:43:44.160 --> 00:43:44.993
Right?

00:43:44.993 --> 00:43:49.440
And part of what I was
trying to do at the time,

00:43:49.440 --> 00:43:51.920
and I failed miserably on certain levels,

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:55.290
was to really to get him
to understand himself

00:43:55.290 --> 00:43:58.900
in relation to others, right?

00:43:58.900 --> 00:44:02.480
Not to let the anger take root in here,

00:44:02.480 --> 00:44:06.180
but to really use it as a way to motivate

00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:08.230
changing the world as such.

00:44:08.230 --> 00:44:12.410
And again, that requires this
I-thou relationship, right?

00:44:12.410 --> 00:44:15.970
That requires to, for
him to think of himself

00:44:15.970 --> 00:44:19.460
in more expansive terms and the like.

00:44:19.460 --> 00:44:20.970
But you know, it was a challenge, doc.

00:44:20.970 --> 00:44:22.770
It was a challenge.

00:44:22.770 --> 00:44:25.270
'Cause we both were pacing like panthers,

00:44:25.270 --> 00:44:27.241
to be honest with you.

00:44:27.241 --> 00:44:29.000
- Yeah, and I appreciate your honesty too

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:31.517
and voicing the challenge

00:44:31.517 --> 00:44:34.200
and what you had to help him walk through.

00:44:34.200 --> 00:44:36.780
I mean, I'm thinking of
just so many folks who,

00:44:36.780 --> 00:44:38.500
you know, we've been doing
that in so many different ways,

00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:41.117
and I would say the one thing
that's been really encouraging

00:44:41.117 --> 00:44:43.040
and this community idea
keeps coming up too,

00:44:43.040 --> 00:44:45.820
but the way that people were able to

00:44:47.030 --> 00:44:49.027
not only just internalize
it but to externalize it

00:44:49.027 --> 00:44:51.227
and to build community with that.

00:44:51.227 --> 00:44:52.780
So you know even within neuroscience,

00:44:52.780 --> 00:44:54.530
by the time this airs this
will be a couple of weeks out,

00:44:54.530 --> 00:44:58.000
but there's a black and neuro
event that's been going on.

00:44:58.000 --> 00:45:01.320
That started from one
student's tweet a year ago.

00:45:01.320 --> 00:45:03.980
Now they have 20,000 followers on Twitter.

00:45:03.980 --> 00:45:06.850
They've done 56 events in the last year.

00:45:06.850 --> 00:45:08.210
And just the way that they've been able

00:45:08.210 --> 00:45:11.880
to galvanize a community,
as students, as trainees,

00:45:11.880 --> 00:45:13.310
because they took that pain

00:45:13.310 --> 00:45:14.910
and tried to build something with it,

00:45:14.910 --> 00:45:17.500
rather than just getting
stuck in themselves

00:45:17.500 --> 00:45:19.050
even though that would be so easy to do.

00:45:19.050 --> 00:45:21.487
But just, not that it's
a solution to everything,

00:45:21.487 --> 00:45:23.670
but there's been so much that's happened

00:45:23.670 --> 00:45:25.210
because of that movement.

00:45:25.210 --> 00:45:27.000
People who, you know, all
the things that happen

00:45:27.000 --> 00:45:28.980
with people being in
predominantly white spaces

00:45:28.980 --> 00:45:30.150
feeling like they're the only one,

00:45:30.150 --> 00:45:32.310
like people being able
to say, oh, I didn't know

00:45:32.310 --> 00:45:33.707
there was this person here,
there's this person here,

00:45:33.707 --> 00:45:36.090
and being able to connect in that way

00:45:36.090 --> 00:45:37.990
and just what that has been in terms of

00:45:37.990 --> 00:45:39.730
just people's day to day,

00:45:39.730 --> 00:45:41.400
the encouragement
they've gotten from that.

00:45:41.400 --> 00:45:43.210
So I think, you know that,
to me that's been just

00:45:43.210 --> 00:45:46.400
a great example of seeing the
young people just rising up

00:45:46.400 --> 00:45:50.148
and moving forward in
the midst of the pain.

00:45:50.148 --> 00:45:52.070
- That's wonderful.
- It's been encouraging.

00:45:52.070 --> 00:45:54.253
- All right, here you go, I got you.

00:45:55.100 --> 00:46:00.100
First step, you gotta know
history and know your history.

00:46:01.550 --> 00:46:02.740
I think that's very important.

00:46:02.740 --> 00:46:05.390
Even down to the fact of, I,

00:46:05.390 --> 00:46:06.670
when I started taking this journey,

00:46:06.670 --> 00:46:09.580
I started asking my mom, what happened,

00:46:09.580 --> 00:46:12.480
what was happening to you while
you were pregnant with me?

00:46:13.750 --> 00:46:15.420
A lot of people don't know those things.

00:46:15.420 --> 00:46:19.640
And I uncovered a key
story, 'cause my life

00:46:19.640 --> 00:46:23.900
has also been marked by
everything just felt like a fight.

00:46:23.900 --> 00:46:25.850
Though I grew up in Detroit
and things were crazy,

00:46:25.850 --> 00:46:27.140
everything just felt like a fight.

00:46:27.140 --> 00:46:29.170
Like why does everything feel so hard?

00:46:29.170 --> 00:46:31.560
And she told me a story
recently that shocked my mind.

00:46:31.560 --> 00:46:34.750
She goes, well son, I was
waiting to tell you this,

00:46:34.750 --> 00:46:35.943
you know, when your mom says, oh, okay

00:46:35.943 --> 00:46:37.693
what she about to tell me (laughs)?

00:46:39.190 --> 00:46:42.300
She says at the time medicine

00:46:42.300 --> 00:46:44.400
wasn't as advanced as it is now,

00:46:44.400 --> 00:46:48.070
and a doctor turned and
said to me, she says,

00:46:48.070 --> 00:46:50.750
if your son doesn't figure out

00:46:50.750 --> 00:46:52.440
how to come through this canal,

00:46:52.440 --> 00:46:54.610
'cause we can't grab
him or we'll kill him,

00:46:54.610 --> 00:46:59.100
if he doesn't figure out how
to come through this canal,

00:46:59.100 --> 00:47:00.453
you both will die.

00:47:02.530 --> 00:47:04.130
Think about that.

00:47:04.130 --> 00:47:07.930
So I go that makes sense.

00:47:07.930 --> 00:47:12.650
She said, so you've had to
figure out things with no manual

00:47:12.650 --> 00:47:15.080
even before you took your first breath.

00:47:15.080 --> 00:47:18.460
- Wow.
- Now that explains a lot.

00:47:18.460 --> 00:47:19.837
So I'm like thank you for the story mom

00:47:19.837 --> 00:47:21.260
but why you ain't tell me this earlier?

00:47:21.260 --> 00:47:24.743
But praise God, you know?
(all laughing)

00:47:25.579 --> 00:47:30.010
But that gives context now to

00:47:30.010 --> 00:47:32.310
why life has felt like a struggle

00:47:32.310 --> 00:47:34.280
though there's been triumphs.

00:47:34.280 --> 00:47:36.230
Those are the stories we need to uncover

00:47:36.230 --> 00:47:38.400
in our personal lives
and our historical lives.

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:43.320
I'll give you another that
helped me from Ida B. Wells.

00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:46.580
When I realized her suffrage,

00:47:46.580 --> 00:47:49.020
not only trying to get
people to understand

00:47:49.020 --> 00:47:51.360
the tragedy of lynching
in the white church,

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:54.100
but then the pushback she
got in the black church,

00:47:54.100 --> 00:47:57.960
I go, oh, this helps me understand

00:47:57.960 --> 00:47:59.770
what we would be up against,

00:47:59.770 --> 00:48:03.130
even in showing people
the humanity of truth

00:48:03.130 --> 00:48:04.820
some people are going, yeah,

00:48:04.820 --> 00:48:07.463
we just want to stay undercover though.

00:48:08.390 --> 00:48:10.270
Don't shine the light on the truth

00:48:10.270 --> 00:48:14.947
'cause it may affect the thing
we're surviving with, right?

00:48:16.270 --> 00:48:17.890
So those things help.

00:48:17.890 --> 00:48:20.680
So your history, understand history,

00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:22.140
and then the last thing I would say

00:48:22.140 --> 00:48:24.630
that people don't want to do,
and you got to do it daily,

00:48:24.630 --> 00:48:27.603
they don't want to name
the things that pain them.

00:48:29.750 --> 00:48:32.510
So that could be in a daily thing like

00:48:32.510 --> 00:48:35.440
hey babe, when you said that, that hurt.

00:48:35.440 --> 00:48:37.490
Hey son, when you treated me like that

00:48:37.490 --> 00:48:39.320
in front of the other parents, that hurt.

00:48:39.320 --> 00:48:40.430
I remember I had to have a conversation

00:48:40.430 --> 00:48:42.610
with my daughter as, I think
she was eight years old,

00:48:42.610 --> 00:48:45.410
we were at a tennis
tournament, and I didn't react

00:48:45.410 --> 00:48:47.780
but I pulled it to her
side, I said, babe, hey,

00:48:47.780 --> 00:48:49.760
the way you're treating me,

00:48:49.760 --> 00:48:52.450
it feels like I'm doing
nothing for you as a parent.

00:48:52.450 --> 00:48:53.293
That hurts.

00:48:54.413 --> 00:48:57.232
Now, is that where I come from?

00:48:57.232 --> 00:48:58.065
No!

00:48:58.065 --> 00:48:59.693
I had to learn that.

00:48:59.693 --> 00:49:01.170
But that helped her understand

00:49:01.170 --> 00:49:03.470
that dad is a person in his emotions,

00:49:03.470 --> 00:49:06.130
and then that helps
her know how to process

00:49:06.130 --> 00:49:07.750
and flesh out her emotions.

00:49:07.750 --> 00:49:09.904
- Wow.
- That's so good.

00:49:09.904 --> 00:49:11.300
I was gonna say, that
took a lot of bravery

00:49:11.300 --> 00:49:13.630
on your part too.
- Indeed.

00:49:13.630 --> 00:49:15.510
- And not be the black parent like, oh,

00:49:15.510 --> 00:49:18.010
oh so this is what you got?
(all laughing)

00:49:18.010 --> 00:49:19.560
- Bring your little, come here!

00:49:20.800 --> 00:49:24.043
Snatch you off the court.
(all laughing)

00:49:26.400 --> 00:49:27.870
- But there's so much
teaching that you did

00:49:27.870 --> 00:49:30.090
in that moment too.
- Absolutely.

00:49:30.090 --> 00:49:30.990
- For her.

00:49:30.990 --> 00:49:34.184
Just to be able to show that example

00:49:34.184 --> 00:49:35.734
of the importance of naming it.

00:49:36.760 --> 00:49:38.610
Being able to address it head on, so.

00:49:39.660 --> 00:49:41.480
Yeah, so good.

00:49:41.480 --> 00:49:43.920
Well, I'm not leaving much
time to talk about the country,

00:49:43.920 --> 00:49:46.750
but I did want to pull
that in just a little bit

00:49:46.750 --> 00:49:49.240
in terms of, I mean, all the things

00:49:49.240 --> 00:49:52.740
that we've brought up that
I think are so important

00:49:52.740 --> 00:49:55.020
and talking about those
aspects of community

00:49:55.020 --> 00:49:57.070
and what we do, staying in our lane,

00:49:57.070 --> 00:49:59.090
our interaction, spheres of influence.

00:49:59.090 --> 00:50:02.663
But when it comes to
the country as a whole,

00:50:04.450 --> 00:50:08.843
where are we in this state of denial

00:50:09.900 --> 00:50:12.370
versus really trying
to move things forward?

00:50:12.370 --> 00:50:13.270
'Cause I know we don't have time

00:50:13.270 --> 00:50:14.390
to get into all the details,

00:50:14.390 --> 00:50:16.640
but even all the conversations
around critical race theory,

00:50:16.640 --> 00:50:19.520
which of course it's based
more on the law school setting

00:50:19.520 --> 00:50:22.150
seems to be getting
blown up and pushed back

00:50:22.150 --> 00:50:23.350
and all these different types of things

00:50:23.350 --> 00:50:24.660
and Eddie I've heard
you say that, you know,

00:50:24.660 --> 00:50:26.040
some of the pushback has been an attempt

00:50:26.040 --> 00:50:27.560
to arrest what we thought

00:50:27.560 --> 00:50:30.580
was a genuine racial
reckoning in this country.

00:50:30.580 --> 00:50:31.780
So I'm just curious to
know, as we wrap up,

00:50:31.780 --> 00:50:33.630
like what your, just
what your thoughts are

00:50:33.630 --> 00:50:34.900
about where we are as a country

00:50:34.900 --> 00:50:37.940
and what we really need
to do to move this forward

00:50:37.940 --> 00:50:39.300
as we talked about the personal community,

00:50:39.300 --> 00:50:42.488
but how do we change
where we are as a nation?

00:50:42.488 --> 00:50:45.150
- Well, I think, you
know, we're in real time

00:50:45.150 --> 00:50:47.150
experiencing the reassertion of the lie.

00:50:48.630 --> 00:50:50.233
At every single time,
at every single moment

00:50:50.233 --> 00:50:51.830
when the nation is on the cusp

00:50:51.830 --> 00:50:55.340
of fundamentally grappling
with the central contradiction

00:50:55.340 --> 00:50:58.740
that threatens democracy, the nation,

00:50:58.740 --> 00:51:00.200
white people in particular,

00:51:00.200 --> 00:51:02.183
they double down on the ugliness,

00:51:03.250 --> 00:51:06.649
and we're seeing it in
real time right now.

00:51:06.649 --> 00:51:08.080
You know, I'm from the
coast of Mississippi

00:51:08.080 --> 00:51:10.080
so I know a little bit about hurricanes.

00:51:10.960 --> 00:51:13.710
You know, the front end of
a hurricane is very violent,

00:51:14.840 --> 00:51:17.820
tears up everything, but
then there's the eye,

00:51:17.820 --> 00:51:19.230
and then you come, the
eye allows you to come out

00:51:20.430 --> 00:51:23.870
and assess the damage,
but the tail is coming

00:51:25.400 --> 00:51:27.400
and the tail is as bad as the front end.

00:51:28.380 --> 00:51:29.930
The tail is still coming y'all.

00:51:30.930 --> 00:51:35.910
So we're at a crossroads and I'm not sure

00:51:35.910 --> 00:51:39.010
if we double down on
the ugliness this time,

00:51:39.010 --> 00:51:41.010
whether or not the nation will survive,

00:51:41.010 --> 00:51:42.313
to be honest with you.

00:51:43.873 --> 00:51:46.910
- And what do you, give
me, need some thoughts.

00:51:46.910 --> 00:51:49.540
When you say double down on the ugliness?

00:51:49.540 --> 00:51:51.840
- The idea that this
must be a white nation

00:51:51.840 --> 00:51:53.770
in the vein of old Europe.

00:51:53.770 --> 00:51:55.810
So they're gonna disenfranchise folk,

00:51:55.810 --> 00:51:59.120
they're gonna, you know,
at the moment in which

00:51:59.120 --> 00:52:02.113
we were trying to address
policing in the country,

00:52:03.660 --> 00:52:05.840
passing just something
that was really baseline,

00:52:05.840 --> 00:52:08.550
the George Floyd Justice and Policing Act,

00:52:08.550 --> 00:52:10.830
they're already trying to
pull back qualified immunity.

00:52:10.830 --> 00:52:12.027
They're trying to gut it.

00:52:12.027 --> 00:52:14.630
And at the same time that
they're trying to gut it,

00:52:14.630 --> 00:52:17.580
they're now trafficking in the language

00:52:17.580 --> 00:52:20.610
of the increase in violent
crime as a justification

00:52:20.610 --> 00:52:23.080
to redouble the efforts that led

00:52:23.080 --> 00:52:25.240
to the carceral state in the first place.

00:52:25.240 --> 00:52:30.240
So we're in the midst of a
reassertion of a way of being

00:52:32.320 --> 00:52:33.880
and the people who are doing it

00:52:33.880 --> 00:52:35.710
don't give a damn about democracy.

00:52:35.710 --> 00:52:38.173
So the two of them,
these things converging,

00:52:39.230 --> 00:52:41.210
I don't know if this place will survive,

00:52:41.210 --> 00:52:43.230
to be honest with you, if they do it.

00:52:43.230 --> 00:52:45.729
I don't know if we will survive
it, to be honest with you.

00:52:45.729 --> 00:52:46.562
- No, it's good.

00:52:46.562 --> 00:52:48.273
You know, it's interesting you say that,

00:52:48.273 --> 00:52:50.500
'cause a friend's been on my head to watch

00:52:50.500 --> 00:52:53.930
this docu-series called
How to Become a Dictator,

00:52:53.930 --> 00:52:57.030
How to be a Dictator on Netflix.

00:52:57.030 --> 00:53:01.540
In episode one talks exactly
about what you're talking about

00:53:01.540 --> 00:53:05.560
and why dictator's rise is because

00:53:05.560 --> 00:53:09.463
they say what a lot of people
actually feel and want.

00:53:12.060 --> 00:53:13.560
And you're seeing it 'cause, you know,

00:53:13.560 --> 00:53:16.580
people are like, man, how is this?

00:53:16.580 --> 00:53:18.120
And they go, no, it's been there.

00:53:18.120 --> 00:53:21.020
They just needed someone
they can get behind.

00:53:21.020 --> 00:53:23.493
- Exactly.
- And that's where we are.

00:53:26.420 --> 00:53:29.840
- Yeah, but you know,
as Baldwin would say,

00:53:29.840 --> 00:53:32.823
human beings are miracles and
disasters at the same time.

00:53:33.720 --> 00:53:34.880
We have to protect ourselves

00:53:34.880 --> 00:53:36.550
from the disasters that we've become,

00:53:36.550 --> 00:53:39.910
but if we show up, if we all
show up and risk everything,

00:53:39.910 --> 00:53:41.240
there's a chance for a miracle.

00:53:41.240 --> 00:53:43.240
So we just need to show up.

00:53:43.240 --> 00:53:44.745
- Yeah, and that's good.

00:53:44.745 --> 00:53:45.870
I mean that was gonna be my next question,

00:53:45.870 --> 00:53:49.440
do we have hope in the
midst of everything?

00:53:49.440 --> 00:53:51.694
- Yeah, you know, Baldwin,
I'm quoting Baldwin again,

00:53:51.694 --> 00:53:54.600
Jimmy has this wonderful line, he says,

00:53:54.600 --> 00:53:55.590
he has this wonderful line, doc,

00:53:55.590 --> 00:53:57.340
he says hope is invented every day.

00:53:58.700 --> 00:54:00.480
And if hope is invented
every day that means

00:54:00.480 --> 00:54:02.933
you've beaten back despair every day.

00:54:03.910 --> 00:54:05.959
So yeah, yeah, we got hope.

00:54:05.959 --> 00:54:07.330
We got to have hope in us.

00:54:07.330 --> 00:54:09.680
We're not naive, it's a
blues soaked hope, doc.

00:54:10.520 --> 00:54:11.867
- I'll take it, I'll take it.

00:54:11.867 --> 00:54:14.140
And to pull in the psychology
and neuroscience again,

00:54:14.140 --> 00:54:15.600
just 'cause that's who I am-

00:54:15.600 --> 00:54:16.433
- Come on man, bring it in.

00:54:16.433 --> 00:54:17.740
- I think there's so
much evidence from that,

00:54:17.740 --> 00:54:20.240
you know, as people, if we
just talk about therapy,

00:54:20.240 --> 00:54:22.560
if people are going through
therapy, if they have hope,

00:54:22.560 --> 00:54:25.250
they're that much more
likely to get through

00:54:25.250 --> 00:54:26.740
and get to a place of healing.

00:54:26.740 --> 00:54:28.900
Maybe not wholeness or complete healing,

00:54:28.900 --> 00:54:31.650
but to actually be able to
move through in that place

00:54:31.650 --> 00:54:33.370
and then from a neuroscience perspective,

00:54:33.370 --> 00:54:34.820
just that expectation,

00:54:34.820 --> 00:54:35.957
what that does to our brains

00:54:35.957 --> 00:54:38.850
and the way our brains
process different situations.

00:54:38.850 --> 00:54:41.817
So I think that hope is
key in a lot of ways.

00:54:41.817 --> 00:54:43.340
And there's just so much evidence for that

00:54:43.340 --> 00:54:45.920
so it's something that we gotta hold onto.

00:54:45.920 --> 00:54:47.450
Maybe I need to start pulling in Baldwin

00:54:47.450 --> 00:54:49.880
in some of my neuroscience courses too.

00:54:49.880 --> 00:54:51.810
Tie those two together.
(all laughing)

00:54:51.810 --> 00:54:53.377
- What are you thinking, A.D.?

00:54:53.377 --> 00:54:54.210
What do you think?

00:54:54.210 --> 00:54:59.130
- Yeah, you know, I'm
always gonna default to

00:55:00.290 --> 00:55:04.650
a hope that's tangible
and a hope that's honest.

00:55:04.650 --> 00:55:07.200
And I think in order for us to survive,

00:55:07.200 --> 00:55:09.000
we have to have an honest hope

00:55:09.930 --> 00:55:14.930
and I would agree with Eddie
that the lie is resurfacing,

00:55:16.570 --> 00:55:19.630
and sometimes you have
the lie of a false hope,

00:55:19.630 --> 00:55:23.020
and that's what becomes
dangerous, you know?

00:55:23.020 --> 00:55:26.210
Now I'm gonna say this
as a person of color

00:55:26.210 --> 00:55:31.210
and you know, on the heels
of reading Caste, the book,

00:55:32.470 --> 00:55:37.470
I actually think people
who know true oppression

00:55:37.560 --> 00:55:40.210
